cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Evidence-Based Prompt Engineering"
message: "If you use this reference, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Aporta Franco"
    given-names: "Fernando"
    alias: ferinazumaDEV
repository-code: "https://github.com/ferinazumaDEV/prompt-engineering-evidence"
url: "https://zentimes.es"
# Concept DOI: resolves to the latest archived release, so it does not change when a new
# version is tagged. Version DOIs live on the Zenodo record page, not here.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22307826"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22307826"
    description: "Concept DOI — always the latest version"
abstract: >-
  A prompt-engineering reference in which every technique is graded solid, mixed or
  folklore, and every claim names a primary source. The evidence lives in a
  machine-readable ledger. This first release grades eight techniques (three solid,
  two mixed, three folklore) and ships one offline reproducible experiment; it is a
  sourced starting corpus, not an exhaustive survey. Effects that can only be
  measured with live model calls are recorded as dated snapshots against a named
  model, never as standing truths.
# ONE license id here, deliberately. This repository IS multi-licensed — prose
# CC BY-SA 4.0 (root LICENSE), the ledger under data/ and the templates CC BY 4.0,
# code MIT (LICENSES/MIT.txt) — and README.md is where that is stated. Do not
# restore the list. This file is what Zenodo reads to archive a release, and its
# deposit format takes a single license id: a YAML list here converts to
# {"id": ["CC-BY-SA-4.0", "CC-BY-4.0", "MIT"]}, Zenodo rejects the deposit, and the
# release is archived as Failed with no DOI minted. That is not a guess — it is what
# happened to the cookbook's v0.1.0 on 4 Sep 2026, reproduced with cffconvert 2.0.0.
# Keep this value equal to the root LICENSE, which is also what GitHub detects.
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
keywords:
  - prompt engineering
  - large language models
  - evidence-based
  - prompt injection
  - LLM evaluation
# version and date-released must be bumped together with the git tag; the tag is the
# source of truth, these two are the copy CFF requires.
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-09-04"
